00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:19.930
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: So you
know, the other day

00:03:19.930 --> 00:03:22.630
we were talking about the
denial of service attacks.

00:03:22.630 --> 00:03:25.150
And a good way to
essentially slice

00:03:25.150 --> 00:03:28.330
these denial of service
attacks is, think of them

00:03:28.330 --> 00:03:30.380
as being in separate buckets.

00:03:30.380 --> 00:03:33.250
The first one is the
infrastructure DDoS attacks.

00:03:33.250 --> 00:03:35.620
So this is where an attacker
will try and send you

00:03:35.620 --> 00:03:38.780
huge volumes of traffic
and will try and overwhelm

00:03:38.780 --> 00:03:40.210
your application.

00:03:40.210 --> 00:03:44.400
So examples include TCP
SYN floods, DNS, NTP

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:46.570
and other amplification
attacks, things

00:03:46.570 --> 00:03:49.670
such as IP fragmentation
attacks and so on.

00:03:49.670 --> 00:03:54.157
These literally require tons and
tons of bandwidth to mitigate.

00:03:54.157 --> 00:03:55.990
Now, there's another
more sophisticated form

00:03:55.990 --> 00:03:58.670
of attacks, which are called
application-aware attacks.

00:03:58.670 --> 00:04:01.960
These not necessarily
depend on volume,

00:04:01.960 --> 00:04:04.510
but they depend on being
aware of the application

00:04:04.510 --> 00:04:07.300
and then exploiting the
weakness of the application.

00:04:07.300 --> 00:04:09.910
So examples would be Slowloris,
cross-site scripting,

00:04:09.910 --> 00:04:12.770
SQL injection, and so on.

00:04:12.770 --> 00:04:16.000
And then the last one would
be, a lot of these attackers

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:17.980
are very sophisticated,
and they're essentially

00:04:17.980 --> 00:04:20.260
combining multiple
types of attacks,

00:04:20.260 --> 00:04:22.675
and then that's what we
call the multivector attack.

00:04:22.675 --> 00:04:24.550
NICK ORLOFF: I love that
Google is protecting

00:04:24.550 --> 00:04:26.680
not only the infrastructure
itself, but also

00:04:26.680 --> 00:04:28.316
the applications.

00:04:28.316 --> 00:04:30.190
Would you mind going
into, just real briefly,

00:04:30.190 --> 00:04:33.065
what cross-site scripting
and SQL injection are?

00:04:33.065 --> 00:04:33.940
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: Sure.

00:04:33.940 --> 00:04:36.790
So as I mentioned
before, the attacker

00:04:36.790 --> 00:04:39.640
depends on knowing something
about the application.

00:04:39.640 --> 00:04:41.650
In both cases, the
attacker is trying

00:04:41.650 --> 00:04:44.410
to inject some malicious
code and making

00:04:44.410 --> 00:04:46.720
that code execute,
because that's

00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:48.470
a weakness in the application.

00:04:48.470 --> 00:04:51.460
So for example, in
case of SQL injection,

00:04:51.460 --> 00:04:54.550
the attacker is trying to
take the flow of the SQL

00:04:54.550 --> 00:04:57.470
and trying to execute
queries against the database.

00:04:57.470 --> 00:05:00.190
So imagine dumping out the
whole database for the attacker.

00:05:00.190 --> 00:05:02.290
That's what you would
do in SQL injection.

00:05:02.290 --> 00:05:04.750
In case of cross-site
scripting, similar, but you're

00:05:04.750 --> 00:05:08.110
essentially injecting malicious
code and making your browser

00:05:08.110 --> 00:05:10.420
execute a set of things
against the application.

00:05:10.420 --> 00:05:13.270
So imagine bypassing access
controls, knobs, and so on.

00:05:13.270 --> 00:05:15.520
So that's what you would do
with cross-site scripting.

00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:17.020
NICK ORLOFF:
Definitely a threat.

00:05:17.020 --> 00:05:19.300
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: For sure.

00:05:19.300 --> 00:05:21.422
So as we started
thinking about, you

