infrastructure that sits

00:10:51.070 --> 00:10:52.760
at the edge of our network.

00:10:52.760 --> 00:10:55.210
And so any time you put
the Global Load Balancer

00:10:55.210 --> 00:10:57.490
in front of your
back ends, which

00:10:57.490 --> 00:10:59.980
is instances or storage
that's sitting inside our data

00:10:59.980 --> 00:11:02.839
centers, you're essentially
stopping the attackers.

00:11:02.839 --> 00:11:04.630
You're stopping the
malicious traffic right

00:11:04.630 --> 00:11:06.460
at the edge of the
network, and you're

00:11:06.460 --> 00:11:08.470
preventing that from
consuming bandwidth

00:11:08.470 --> 00:11:10.420
all the way to your back ends.

00:11:10.420 --> 00:11:13.180
Of course, this is the same
infrastructure that, again,

00:11:13.180 --> 00:11:15.380
defends Google's own services.

00:11:15.380 --> 00:11:18.640
So it is battle tested over
the last decade or more.

00:11:18.640 --> 00:11:20.470
And the last thing
I wanted to mention

00:11:20.470 --> 00:11:24.010
is, Google is a big
believer in TLS everywhere.

00:11:24.010 --> 00:11:26.500
And so generally,
we encourage all

00:11:26.500 --> 00:11:28.480
of our customers to use TLS.

00:11:28.480 --> 00:11:30.430
So you use issue TPS.

00:11:30.430 --> 00:11:32.620
For non-issue TP traffic,
we have other flavors

00:11:32.620 --> 00:11:34.660
of load balancers
that will support it.

00:11:34.660 --> 00:11:37.460
But that is one of the big
things where, you know,

00:11:37.460 --> 00:11:40.860
we've always been a big
proponent of TLS everywhere.

00:11:40.860 --> 00:11:43.950
We introduced seven new
features to help make that easy.

00:11:43.950 --> 00:11:47.710
So things such as SSL
policies, multiple certs,

00:11:47.710 --> 00:11:50.230
we don't charge
anything extra for TLS.

00:11:50.230 --> 00:11:53.380
So it is a huge driver for you
to go and deploy your services

00:11:53.380 --> 00:11:54.674
with encryption turned on.

00:11:54.674 --> 00:11:55.840
NICK ORLOFF: That's awesome.

00:11:55.840 --> 00:11:58.640
And I know that performance
is very critical to Google,

00:11:58.640 --> 00:12:00.640
and so one of the amazing
things that customers

00:12:00.640 --> 00:12:02.284
like about our load
balancing solution

00:12:02.284 --> 00:12:04.450
is that there is absolutely
no pre-warming required.

00:12:04.450 --> 00:12:07.940
Basically, we scale with you
as your traffic scales up.

00:12:07.940 --> 00:12:08.630
Very impressive.

00:12:08.630 --> 00:12:10.630
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: Yeah, and
I think to your point,

00:12:10.630 --> 00:12:13.060
I mean, the reason we
can do all of these

00:12:13.060 --> 00:12:15.910
is, these are just architected
to be massively distributive

00:12:15.910 --> 00:12:20.060
systems, and they benefit from
economies of scale as well.

00:12:20.060 --> 00:12:21.970
So given that, you
know, these can

00:12:21.970 --> 00:12:25.810
handle YouTube worth of traffic,
or search worth of traffic,

00:12:25.810 --> 00:12:29.980
it is very easy for us to also
provide the same scale or even

00:12:29.980 --> 00:12:32.710
the same high availability to
the customers that are actually

00:12:32.710 --> 00:12:33.920
going to use this.

00:12:33.920 --> 00:12:34.670
NICK ORLOFF: Cool.

00:12:37.180 --> 00:12:40.100
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: Which brings
us to the main topic for today,

00:12:40.100 --> 00:12:41.350
which is Cloud Armor.

00:12:41.350 --> 00:12:43.630
So we spoke about
the network and we

00:12:43.630 --> 00:12:46.060
spoke about the
Global Load Balancer

00:12:46.060 --> 00:12:47.860
as being your first
line of defense,

00:12:47.860 --> 00:12:50.200
including against
volumetric and protocol

00:12:50.200 --> 00:12:53.285
attack, so your
infrastructure DDoS defense.

00:12:53.285 --> 00:12:56.080
When we provided these
two, we used to often hear

00:12:56.080 --> 00:12:57.030
from our customers.

00:12:57.030 --> 00:12:58.810
So is there a way
for me to go put

00:12:58.810 --> 00:13:00.130
a blacklist or a white list?

00:13:00.130 --> 00:13:03.310
Or can I go control
access to my service

00:13:03.310 --> 00:13:04.940
if it's coming
from a certain geo?

00:13:04.940 --> 00:13:06.640
Can I go drop that traffic?

00:13:06.640 --> 00:13:10.590
Or can you help me put
some sort of WAF rules?

00:13:10.590 --> 00:13:14.330
As we started hearing more
and more of those requests,

00:13:14.330 --> 00:13:16.970
that is how this product,
Cloud Armor, was born.

00:13:16.970 --> 00:13:18.760
We also used to hear
from our partners

00:13:18.760 --> 00:13:20.800
about the requirement
for something like this.

00:13:20.800 --> 00:13:22.850
We'll deep dive into that.

00:13:22.850 --> 00:13:25.450
So again, when we
say whether it's

00:13:25.450 --> 00:13:27.730
the global load balance
or our Cloud Armor,

00:13:27.730 --> 00:13:29.860
the infrastructure
that is used to deliver

00:13:29.860 --> 00:13:31.780
these products
exists, has existed

00:13:31.780 --> 00:13:34.520
for decades, a decade or more.

00:13:34.520 --> 00:13:36.970
And what we give you is a
way to go and orchestrate

00:13:36.970 --> 00:13:38.560
this infrastructure
so you can use it

00:13:38.560 --> 00:13:40.400
to protect your own services.

00:13:40.400 --> 00:13:42.520
So it's a really
good combination

00:13:42.520 --> 00:13:44.860
where you have Google's
global network.

00:13:44.860 --> 00:13:46.450
You have the cloud
load balancer.

00:13:46.450 --> 00:13:48.910
And then Cloud
Armor is a service

00:13:48.910 --> 00:13:51.940
that today is supported with
HTTP/HTTPS load balancer,

00:13:51.940 --> 00:13:54.740
will be supported with
other services, as well.

00:13:54.740 --> 00:13:58.300
So the other key thing
is it is basically

00:13:58.300 --> 00:14:01.630
defense at scale, which
means you don't have

00:14:01.630 --> 00:14:03.890
to worry about how
many queries per second

00:14:03.890 --> 00:14:05.570
this Cloud Armor
thing can support,

00:14:05.570 --> 00:14:09.790
because the thing itself is so
distributed that it will scale

00:14:09.790 --> 00:14:10.990
up based on your traffic.

00:14:10.990 --> 00:14:14.085
So you don't have to worry about
the HE of Cloud Armor itself.

00:14:14.085 --> 00:14:15.460
And then the last
thing we wanted

00:14:15.460 --> 00:14:18.670
to build is essentially a
policy-driven framework,

00:14:18.670 --> 00:14:21.580
so build a very robust
policy-driven framework

00:14:21.580 --> 00:14:24.760
that then you can go build on
to deliver security policies

00:14:24.760 --> 00:14:27.910
or block certain type of things,
or even build your own custom

00:14:27.910 --> 00:14:29.120
rules, and so on.

00:14:29.120 --> 00:14:32.590
So that, in a nutshell,
is Cloud Armor.

00:14:32.590 --> 00:14:34.870
One of the things that
I also mentioned briefly

00:14:34.870 --> 00:14:38.320
is that it works with the
global HTTP/HTTPS load balancer.

00:14:38.320 --> 00:14:40.850
So that's what you
can see in here,

00:14:40.850 --> 00:14:42.700
which means that the
infrastructure DDoS

00:14:42.700 --> 00:14:45.730
defense is taken care of by
the global HTTP/HTTPS load

00:14:45.730 --> 00:14:46.810
balancer.

00:14:46.810 --> 00:14:48.610
And then several of
the other capabilities

00:14:48.610 --> 00:14:53.800
that we spoke about will be
taken care of by Cloud Armor.

00:14:53.800 --> 00:14:56.260
But one of the things I
thought is, it's one thing

00:14:56.260 --> 00:14:58.120
to talk about Cloud Armor.

00:14:58.120 --> 00:14:59.620
Why don't we go
show it in action?

00:14:59.620 --> 00:15:00.370
Should we do that?

00:15:00.370 --> 00:15:01.120
NICK ORLOVE: Yeah,
let's do that.

00:15:01.120 --> 00:15:01.510
I'm excited.

00:15:01.510 --> 00:15:02.301
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: OK.

00:15:10.490 --> 00:15:12.120
Hi, everyone.

00:15:12.120 --> 00:15:15.000
Welcome to our demo
on denial of service.

00:15:15.000 --> 00:15:18.000
Today we're going to show
how a top retailer defends

00:15:18.000 --> 00:15:20.700
against denial of
service attack.

00:15:20.700 --> 00:15:23.490
Today is Black Friday and
we have some excellent deals

00:15:23.490 --> 00:15:24.690
on our web site.

00:15:24.690 --> 00:15:28.050
And we have a number of shoppers
who are up early on the morning

00:15:28.050 --> 00:15:30.090
to shop on our website.

00:15:30.090 --> 00:15:33.420
The boxes represent our happy
shoppers across the globe.

00:15:33.420 --> 00:15:36.710
And they're shopping on our
web site and buying products.

00:15:36.710 --> 00:15:39.780
Now, if you look at this
one, this is essentially

00:15:39.780 --> 00:15:41.340
our shopping app.

00:15:41.340 --> 00:15:43.410
And the baskets
essentially are servicing

00:15:43.410 --> 00:15:45.970
all of the transactions
that are coming in.

00:15:45.970 --> 00:15:48.960
You can see the lovely boxes
floating in across the globe,

00:15:48.960 --> 00:15:50.600
coming into our service.

00:15:50.600 --> 00:15:52.320
And you can see a
bunch of happy shoppers

00:15:52.320 --> 00:15:55.210
who bought some
really great deals.

00:15:55.210 --> 00:15:58.380
Here's the thing-- this
is also prime opportunity

00:15:58.380 --> 00:16:02.380
for an attacker to go launch
an infrastructure DDoS attack.

00:16:02.380 --> 00:16:03.270
Look at the screen.

00:16:03.270 --> 00:16:05.800
You can see some really
happy shoppers shopping.

00:16:05.800 --> 00:16:08.460
But with infrastructure
DDoS attack,

00:16:08.460 --> 00:16:10.290
the attacker sends
in tons and tons

00:16:10.290 --> 00:16:13.696
of traffic trying to overwhelm
our shopping service.

00:16:13.696 --> 00:16:16.070
And in fact, if you look at
some of the largest attacks

00:16:16.070 --> 00:16:18.000
that we've seen
at Google, you can

00:16:18.000 --> 00:16:21.000
see that they are increasing
exponentially in volume.

00:16:21.000 --> 00:16:24.500
And in fact, if you look
at all of these attacks,

00:16:24.500 --> 00:16:27.860
they are like watching half
a million YouTube videos all

00:16:27.860 --> 00:16:30.780
at the same time
in high-definition.

00:16:30.780 --> 00:16:32.622
Look at those attack
packets coming in.

00:16:32.622 --> 00:16:34.080
They would have
totally overwhelmed

00:16:34.080 --> 00:16:35.430
our shopping service.

00:16:35.430 --> 00:16:38.370
And our shoppers would not
be able to shop at all.

00:16:38.370 --> 00:16:40.080
But we were prepared for this.

00:16:40.080 --> 00:16:43.040
Prior to Black Friday,
we have deployed

00:16:43.040 --> 00:16:45.270
Google global load balancing.

00:16:45.270 --> 00:16:47.550
You can see the
beautiful blue ring.

00:16:47.550 --> 00:16:49.800
This is Google's
global infrastructure

00:16:49.800 --> 00:16:51.840
that's protecting
our shopping app

00:16:51.840 --> 00:16:55.727
and making sure that all of
our customers can shop happily.

00:16:55.727 --> 00:16:57.560
You can see that all
the infrastructure DDoS

00:16:57.560 --> 00:17:01.750
attacks are mitigated, and
we have some happy shoppers.

00:17:01.750 --> 00:17:03.660
However, infrastructure
DDoS attacks

00:17:03.660 --> 00:17:06.600
are not only attacks
you're going to see.

00:17:06.600 --> 00:17:07.328
James?

00:17:07.328 --> 00:17:08.869
JAMES: One of the
other, more common,

00:17:08.869 --> 00:17:11.089
application-level
attacks that we encounter

00:17:11.089 --> 00:17:13.109
is known as SQL injection.

00:17:13.109 --> 00:17:16.490
Now, here in this picture,
you can see the happy packets

00:17:16.490 --> 00:17:18.440
coming in and fulfilling orders.

00:17:18.440 --> 00:17:20.829
But a SQL injection,
once that begins,

00:17:20.829 --> 00:17:24.349
it is problematic for
two important reasons.

00:17:24.349 --> 00:17:25.760
Important reason number one--

00:17:25.760 --> 00:17:27.500
we have a DDoS attack
where it's trying

00:17:27.500 --> 00:17:28.980
to actually deny service.

00:17:28.980 --> 00:17:31.010
But more importantly, in
these types of attacks,

00:17:31.010 --> 00:17:34.230
your data could potentially
also be at risk.

00:17:34.230 --> 00:17:36.710
So coming back here
to our big screen,

00:17:36.710 --> 00:17:38.900
you can see the happy
packets coming in.

00:17:38.900 --> 00:17:41.780
But you can see here the
SQL injection attacks

00:17:41.780 --> 00:17:44.800
coming in and interfering
with the orders on my servers

00:17:44.800 --> 00:17:46.470
as they're going through here.

00:17:46.470 --> 00:17:49.580
Now, both volumetric and
SQL injection attacks

00:17:49.580 --> 00:17:53.100
are things that we can plan for
when we design our application.

00:17:53.100 --> 00:17:56.300
So today we're happy to
announce Cloud Armor.

00:17:56.300 --> 00:17:59.390
Cloud Armor has SQL
injection rules that

00:17:59.390 --> 00:18:02.060
allow us to deploy in advance.

00:18:02.060 --> 00:18:03.920
When this is deployed,
we can go out

00:18:03.920 --> 00:18:05.540
and mitigate these
types of attacks

00:18:05.540 --> 00:18:08.900
before they ever
endanger our application.

00:18:08.900 --> 00:18:11.720
Both volumetric attacks
and SQL injection attacks

00:18:11.720 --> 00:18:14.690
are ones that we can plan for
when we design our application.

00:18:14.690 --> 00:18:17.270
There are more
sophisticated attacks that

00:18:17.270 --> 00:18:19.066
require a reactive approach.

00:18:19.066 --> 00:18:20.690
One of these we're
going to demonstrate

00:18:20.690 --> 00:18:22.231
is going to be a
multi-vector attack.

00:18:24.579 --> 00:18:26.620
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: So one
thing about vector attacks

00:18:26.620 --> 00:18:28.130
are very interesting.

00:18:28.130 --> 00:18:30.430
You have attackers
who use one or more

00:18:30.430 --> 00:18:32.380
or even multiple
types of attacks

00:18:32.380 --> 00:18:34.200
to try and overwhelm
your service.

00:18:34.200 --> 00:18:36.070
So let's look at our service.

00:18:36.070 --> 00:18:38.560
You can see that with our
Black Friday shoppers,

00:18:38.560 --> 00:18:40.430
we have [INAUDIBLE] traffic.

00:18:40.430 --> 00:18:42.520
But we also have
malicious traffic

00:18:42.520 --> 00:18:45.250
that has some interesting
characteristics.

00:18:45.250 --> 00:18:47.320
Look at the bottom
half of the screen.

00:18:47.320 --> 00:18:50.320
What you notice in the
tiny island in the ocean

00:18:50.320 --> 00:18:52.060
is a botnet attack.

00:18:52.060 --> 00:18:54.920
Now, this attack has three
interesting characteristics.

00:18:54.920 --> 00:18:58.120
First, it is originating
from the small island.

00:18:58.120 --> 00:19:00.470
Second, the traffic
that's coming in

00:19:00.470 --> 00:19:02.560
has this really
specific signature

00:19:02.560 --> 00:19:04.750
inside the packet
in the traffic.

00:19:04.750 --> 00:19:07.900
And the third thing is that it's
coming in from a specific type

00:19:07.900 --> 00:19:09.610
of browser.

00:19:09.610 --> 00:19:12.800
We can do something really
useful with this one.

00:19:12.800 --> 00:19:14.730
We talked about Cloud
Armor, which provides

00:19:14.730 --> 00:19:16.990
you application-level defense.

00:19:16.990 --> 00:19:19.930
It also is underpinned by
a very sophisticated rules

00:19:19.930 --> 00:19:22.660
language that lets you
build custom rules.

00:19:22.660 --> 00:19:24.250
And so what we are
going to do now

00:19:24.250 --> 00:19:28.205
to make sure our apps stay
good is to build a custom rule.

00:19:28.205 --> 00:19:30.580
So when we deploy the custom
rule, what's going to happen

00:19:30.580 --> 00:19:32.380
is the Cloud Armor
is going to look

00:19:32.380 --> 00:19:35.460
for a specific pattern, which
is the specific signature,

00:19:35.460 --> 00:19:37.720
the specific browser,
and then also

00:19:37.720 --> 00:19:39.790
ensuring that this is
only traffic coming

00:19:39.790 --> 00:19:41.590
from that specific island.

00:19:41.590 --> 00:19:44.830
This way, you ensure that none
of your legitimate traffic

00:19:44.830 --> 00:19:47.860
is blocked out but
your attackers are.

00:19:47.860 --> 00:19:50.950
So here we launched the
Cloud Armor custom rule.

00:19:50.950 --> 00:19:52.720
You can notice that
the botnet attack has

00:19:52.720 --> 00:19:55.030
been easily mitigated against.

00:19:55.030 --> 00:19:56.680
Everything looks good.

00:19:56.680 --> 00:19:58.220
Our shopping app is happy.

00:19:58.220 --> 00:20:01.720
And most importantly,
our users are happy.

00:20:01.720 --> 00:20:03.670
We have an excellent
Black Friday.

00:20:03.670 --> 00:20:05.600
We've made tons of profit.

00:20:05.600 --> 00:20:07.870
We are now going to go
ship all of those products

00:20:07.870 --> 00:20:09.360
to our happy shoppers.

00:20:09.360 --> 00:20:11.200
And with all that,
we introduced you

00:20:11.200 --> 00:20:13.720
to two interesting products--
the global load balancer

00:20:13.720 --> 00:20:15.310
and Cloud Armor.

00:20:15.310 --> 00:20:18.610
And we have an arsenal of other
products from our partners.

00:20:18.610 --> 00:20:20.530
So with all of
these products, we

00:20:20.530 --> 00:20:23.140
hope you'll build a
comprehensive solution.

00:20:23.140 --> 00:20:25.525
Happy shopping and happy
keeping of your services

00:20:25.525 --> 00:20:26.150
up and running.

00:20:26.150 --> 00:20:26.650
Thank you.

00:20:29.779 --> 00:20:31.570
NICK ORLOVE: And we
are back to being live.

00:20:31.570 --> 00:20:33.520
Well, Prajakta, thanks so
much for showing that video.

00:20:33.520 --> 00:20:36.070
I think it gave a really
good, high-level understanding

00:20:36.070 --> 00:20:38.170
of how Cloud Armor works.

00:20:38.170 --> 00:20:41.470
And I think it demonstrated
how relevant this product is.

00:20:41.470 --> 00:20:45.490
As you saw in the example,
the retailer who basically has

00:20:45.490 --> 00:20:47.837
to deliver a bunch of
products on Black Friday--

00:20:47.837 --> 00:20:50.170
and we all know that Black
Friday can be one of the most

00:20:50.170 --> 00:20:53.500
critical days of the year
for pulling in revenue--

00:20:53.500 --> 00:20:56.380
was able to protect their
services using Cloud Armor.

00:20:56.380 --> 00:20:59.644
So whether you're a retailer or
whether you're a gaming company

00:20:59.644 --> 00:21:01.060
or what have you,
I can definitely

00:21:01.060 --> 00:21:02.476
say that this would
be applicable.

00:21:02.476 --> 00:21:03.677
So very exciting.

00:21:03.677 --> 00:21:06.010
Now, Prajakta, can we go into
some of the nuts and bolts

00:21:06.010 --> 00:21:07.520
of how the policies work?

00:21:07.520 --> 00:21:09.270
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: I knew
you would ask that.

00:21:09.270 --> 00:21:10.540
NICK ORLOVE: I'm sure you did.

00:21:10.540 --> 00:21:11.415
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: Yeah.

00:21:11.415 --> 00:21:14.890
So as you rightly pointed
out, first of all,

00:21:14.890 --> 00:21:17.240
it is critical
for all customers,

00:21:17.240 --> 00:21:19.820
whether you're a retailer,
gaming customer, and so on.

00:21:19.820 --> 00:21:22.900
But what I wanted to do next was
to give a little bit of a peek

00:21:22.900 --> 00:21:26.500
under the hood and explain how
is it that the framework is

00:21:26.500 --> 00:21:28.220
built out, and
especially explain

00:21:28.220 --> 00:21:31.240
by what we mean as a
policy-driven defense,

00:21:31.240 --> 00:21:32.700
or policy-driven DDoS defense.

00:21:32.700 --> 00:21:33.700
NICK ORLOVE: Absolutely.

00:21:33.700 --> 00:21:36.040
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: So the way
Cloud Armor is laid out

00:21:36.040 --> 00:21:38.590
is as a set of policies.

00:21:38.590 --> 00:21:41.960
If you look at this
policy as an example,

00:21:41.960 --> 00:21:45.040
the name of the policy-- there
are three policies in there.

00:21:45.040 --> 00:21:47.650
You can probably read the
three policies in there.

00:21:47.650 --> 00:21:50.860
Now when you think of
a policy, think of it

00:21:50.860 --> 00:21:53.242
as a hierarchy of rules.

00:21:53.242 --> 00:21:54.700
If you look on the
right hand side,

00:21:54.700 --> 00:21:57.370
you can notice that
there is a set of rules.

00:21:57.370 --> 00:22:00.070
At the end is always
a default truth.

00:22:00.070 --> 00:22:04.860
Each rule itself is comprised
of a match condition

00:22:04.860 --> 00:22:06.886
or a set of match conditions.

00:22:06.886 --> 00:22:08.260
And then you'll
have some actions

00:22:08.260 --> 00:22:10.160
that you'll take
on that as well.

00:22:10.160 --> 00:22:12.460
And then you can take
this Cloud Armor policy

00:22:12.460 --> 00:22:15.280
and you can go and connect it
or attach it to one or more

00:22:15.280 --> 00:22:16.610
of your services.

00:22:16.610 --> 00:22:18.070
So the moment you
do that, that's

00:22:18.070 --> 00:22:20.650
when Cloud Armor kicks
in for your services.

00:22:20.650 --> 00:22:23.920
You could choose to have a
custom policy per service,

00:22:23.920 --> 00:22:26.200
or you could essentially
go and put the same policy

00:22:26.200 --> 00:22:27.620
across all of your services.

00:22:27.620 --> 00:22:28.510
So it's up to you.

00:22:28.510 --> 00:22:31.302
NICK ORLOVE: Interesting.

00:22:31.302 --> 00:22:33.260
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: This is
a closer look at what's

00:22:33.260 --> 00:22:34.560
inside a Cloud Armor rule.

00:22:34.560 --> 00:22:37.440
So we spoke about the policy
being comprised of rule.

00:22:37.440 --> 00:22:40.520
Inside the rule, essentially
you have a match condition.

00:22:40.520 --> 00:22:42.980
And this could have
either one match condition

00:22:42.980 --> 00:22:45.380
or there could be a set
of match conditions.

00:22:45.380 --> 00:22:47.600
Once the traffic
matches the signature,

00:22:47.600 --> 00:22:49.310
you obviously want
to tell us what you

00:22:49.310 --> 00:22:50.510
want to do with the traffic.

00:22:50.510 --> 00:22:53.800
So you know, it could be a
variety of actions in there,

00:22:53.800 --> 00:22:55.050
as well.

00:22:55.050 --> 00:22:57.140
You also want to tell us
where this rule should be

00:22:57.140 --> 00:22:59.150
inserted inside the hierarchy.

00:22:59.150 --> 00:23:01.670
So that is what the
priority tells you.

00:23:01.670 --> 00:23:03.690
Preview is, again, another
interesting feature

00:23:03.690 --> 00:23:06.140
where if you enable
preview, then

00:23:06.140 --> 00:23:09.560
whatever action would have been
taken essentially gets locked.

00:23:09.560 --> 00:23:11.820
But the Cloud Armor doesn't
actually take that action.

00:23:11.820 --> 00:23:13.070
NICK ORLOVE: Oh, that's great.

00:23:13.070 --> 00:23:14.540
You can actually see
what's going to happen.

00:23:14.540 --> 00:23:15.110
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: Exactly.

00:23:15.110 --> 00:23:16.820
And imagine you want
to try out a rule.

00:23:16.820 --> 00:23:18.800
So that is when you
would go and configure it

00:23:18.800 --> 00:23:20.030
in the preview mode.

00:23:20.030 --> 00:23:21.160
NICK ORLOVE: OK.

00:23:21.160 --> 00:23:24.080
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: We wanted to
pick a framework for specifying

00:23:24.080 --> 00:23:26.510
these rules using something
that will be open source,

00:23:26.510 --> 00:23:29.240
as well as something
that's in use by some

00:23:29.240 --> 00:23:31.100
of our other security products.

00:23:31.100 --> 00:23:33.530
And that is why we picked
what is called the Common

00:23:33.530 --> 00:23:35.330
Expressions Language, CEL.

00:23:35.330 --> 00:23:38.060
It is hosted on GitHub, so you
can actually take a closer look

00:23:38.060 --> 00:23:39.110
at it, as well.

00:23:39.110 --> 00:23:41.490
But the entire specification
of Cloud Armor rules

00:23:41.490 --> 00:23:42.740
is done using that.

00:23:42.740 --> 00:23:45.890
And Firebase customers, if
you're an existing Firebase

00:23:45.890 --> 00:23:48.166
customer, you've probably
already used it, as well.

00:23:48.166 --> 00:23:50.540
NICK ORLOVE: Yeah, I've got
a couple questions, actually.

00:23:50.540 --> 00:23:51.415
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: Sure.

00:23:51.415 --> 00:23:54.050
NICK ORLOVE: So first being,
I see that the actions either

00:23:54.050 --> 00:23:56.486
allow or deny, which
is a little bit binary.

00:23:56.486 --> 00:23:57.860
And I imagine that
some customers

00:23:57.860 --> 00:24:00.170
would be interested in something
like throttling or rate

00:24:00.170 --> 00:24:00.470
limiting.

00:24:00.470 --> 00:24:02.428
Is that something that
you guys are considering

00:24:02.428 --> 00:24:03.590
in product management?

00:24:03.590 --> 00:24:05.464
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: Yes,
that's a great question.

00:24:05.464 --> 00:24:08.150
So today you can do allow, deny.

00:24:08.150 --> 00:24:10.139
You can also specify
the codes with deny.

00:24:10.139 --> 00:24:12.680
And we are actively working on
adding rate limiting, as well.

00:24:12.680 --> 00:24:12.800
NICK ORLOVE: Awesome.

00:24:12.800 --> 00:24:14.390
I know some of my
customers are going

00:24:14.390 --> 00:24:15.050
to be excited to hear that.

00:24:15.050 --> 00:24:17.330
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: Yeah, I think
it's a pretty useful feature

00:24:17.330 --> 00:24:18.130
for a lot of customers.

00:24:18.130 --> 00:24:19.010
NICK ORLOVE: Cool.

00:24:19.010 --> 00:24:21.350
And then I also wanted to
ask, for the Common Expression

00:24:21.350 --> 00:24:24.410
Language, how complex is that?

00:24:24.410 --> 00:24:26.990
Is that something
that anyone can just

00:24:26.990 --> 00:24:28.650
get going from the get go?

00:24:28.650 --> 00:24:31.360
Or does it involve some
training, some complexity?

00:24:31.360 --> 00:24:33.860
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: Actually, it
is fairly straightforward, very

00:24:33.860 --> 00:24:35.210
intuitive to use.

00:24:35.210 --> 00:24:39.500
And we are also publishing in
the Cloud Armor documentation

00:24:39.500 --> 00:24:42.090
the rules for what you
would typically want to do.

00:24:42.090 --> 00:24:45.260
Having said that, it has a lot
of features which-- actually,

00:24:45.260 --> 00:24:46.710
let me show you what's in here.

00:24:46.710 --> 00:24:47.030
NICK ORLOVE: OK, cool.

00:24:47.030 --> 00:24:49.400
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: So let's
take a closer look at that.

00:24:49.400 --> 00:24:52.340
When you think of Cloud Armor
rules language, the first thing

00:24:52.340 --> 00:24:55.160
is, what are the things to
look for inside the traffic?

00:24:55.160 --> 00:24:56.810
And to specify
those, you typically

00:24:56.810 --> 00:25:00.560
want to look at IP addresses,
whether they are v4 or v6.

00:25:00.560 --> 00:25:03.020
The HTTP headers,
for example, you

00:25:03.020 --> 00:25:05.490
may want to look at the
geolocation of the client.

00:25:05.490 --> 00:25:07.640
So each of these that
you see essentially

00:25:07.640 --> 00:25:11.000
is looking at those specific
things inside the traffic.

00:25:11.000 --> 00:25:13.852
That would be your attribute
or your set of attributes.

00:25:13.852 --> 00:25:15.560
The second thing you
typically want to do

00:25:15.560 --> 00:25:17.330
is combine one or
more attributes.

00:25:17.330 --> 00:25:19.100
And that's where the
operators kick in.

00:25:19.100 --> 00:25:22.590
So imagine this IP
address and not that geo.

00:25:22.590 --> 00:25:26.540
So that's where you use the
operator "and," and also "not."

00:25:26.540 --> 00:25:29.240
And then the last thing is we
have a very sophisticated set

00:25:29.240 --> 00:25:30.100
of operations.

00:25:30.100 --> 00:25:33.210
So for example-- actually,
it's not listed here.

00:25:33.210 --> 00:25:36.050
But you can even do things
such as regular expressions.

00:25:36.050 --> 00:25:38.090
You can do is low
or you can do upper.

00:25:38.090 --> 00:25:40.490
So there's a whole
set of operations

00:25:40.490 --> 00:25:43.250
that you can do to build
out, really, rituals.

00:25:43.250 --> 00:25:45.860
And which ones of
these you use depends

00:25:45.860 --> 00:25:47.880
on what it is that you're
trying to accomplish.

00:25:47.880 --> 00:25:49.421
So that's roughly
what it looks like.

00:25:49.421 --> 00:25:51.380
It's fairly human readable.

00:25:51.380 --> 00:25:54.440
We will provide you an editor
inside our UI, so our Pantheon

00:25:54.440 --> 00:25:55.202
UI.

00:25:55.202 --> 00:25:56.660
And if you want to
automate it, you

00:25:56.660 --> 00:25:59.829
have the gCloud CLI
and API, as well.

00:25:59.829 --> 00:26:00.620
NICK ORLOVE: Great.

00:26:00.620 --> 00:26:02.390
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: And
then as time goes by,

00:26:02.390 --> 00:26:05.390
we'll try and give you
as many pre-canned rules

00:26:05.390 --> 00:26:07.010
as we can so you
don't really have

00:26:07.010 --> 00:26:09.220
to even look at the language
if you choose not to.

00:26:09.220 --> 00:26:10.095
NICK ORLOVE: Love it.

00:26:10.095 --> 00:26:12.362
Cool.

00:26:12.362 --> 00:26:14.070
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: So
let's run through some

00:26:14.070 --> 00:26:17.770
of the use cases that drove
creation of Cloud Armor.

00:26:17.770 --> 00:26:20.430
The most popular use case
that we initially started with

00:26:20.430 --> 00:26:24.450
was essentially a blacklist
and whitelist for your traffic.

00:26:24.450 --> 00:26:28.680
In this example, our customer
wants to only allow, let's say,

00:26:28.680 --> 00:26:31.470
traffic from 9.9.9.0/24.

00:26:31.470 --> 00:26:33.727
So you would put a rule
which looks for that IP

00:26:33.727 --> 00:26:35.310
and then you would
put whatever action

00:26:35.310 --> 00:26:36.690
it is that you want to take.

00:26:36.690 --> 00:26:39.710
This could be either
allow, this could be deny.

00:26:39.710 --> 00:26:42.610
And you could also turn it on
in the preview mode, as well.

00:26:42.610 --> 00:26:45.720
This is supported
both for IPv4 and IPv6

00:26:45.720 --> 00:26:49.600
because our global load balancer
supports IPv6 clients, as well.

00:26:49.600 --> 00:26:53.130
And then this could typically
very likely in many cases

00:26:53.130 --> 00:26:56.250
this specific condition could be
combined with other conditions,

00:26:56.250 --> 00:26:57.910
as well.

00:26:57.910 --> 00:27:01.530
One of the most popular usage
of this specific feature

00:27:01.530 --> 00:27:04.130
is if you're using a
third party service

00:27:04.130 --> 00:27:06.189
or you're using one of
our partner services.

00:27:06.189 --> 00:27:07.980
And let's say you're
scrubbing your traffic

00:27:07.980 --> 00:27:09.890
through the partner.

00:27:09.890 --> 00:27:12.150
Without this feature, even
if you scrub the traffic

00:27:12.150 --> 00:27:14.160
through the partner,
you need something

00:27:14.160 --> 00:27:16.860
at the edge of our network
which says only allow traffic

00:27:16.860 --> 00:27:20.270
in from the partner and deny
all of the traffic that's not

00:27:20.270 --> 00:27:21.760
scrubbed through the partner.

00:27:21.760 --> 00:27:24.420
So this is where you would also
leverage the whitelist feature.

00:27:24.420 --> 00:27:27.610
And that's one of the most
popular use cases for this one.

00:27:27.610 --> 00:27:28.579
NICK ORLOVE: OK.

00:27:28.579 --> 00:27:30.120
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: The
other popular one

00:27:30.120 --> 00:27:32.110
is geobased access control.

00:27:32.110 --> 00:27:34.290
So you want to
bring in essentially

00:27:34.290 --> 00:27:37.600
traffic from certain geos
and block from other geos.

00:27:37.600 --> 00:27:39.570
This is where, again,
you would use Cloud Armor

00:27:39.570 --> 00:27:42.530
with checking for the region
code, which checks for the geo.

00:27:42.530 --> 00:27:45.300
You don't have to worry
about the IPs and so on.

00:27:45.300 --> 00:27:48.720
You just specify the code
which is the ISO alpha 2 code.

00:27:48.720 --> 00:27:51.160
So in this case, it's
using a region code as US.

00:27:51.160 --> 00:27:53.610
So it takes a whole lot
of toil from your end

00:27:53.610 --> 00:27:55.500
in mapping any IPs to geo.

00:27:55.500 --> 00:27:58.170
You just use geo directly.

00:27:58.170 --> 00:28:00.630
The third one, which is, again,
another popular use case,

00:28:00.630 --> 00:28:04.170
is customers asking for
WAF-like functionality,

00:28:04.170 --> 00:28:07.410
more specifically for defense
against application event

00:28:07.410 --> 00:28:10.260
attacks, things such as
cross-site scripting or SQL

00:28:10.260 --> 00:28:12.030
injection that we spoke about.

00:28:12.030 --> 00:28:14.250
And we have pre-canned
rules for it.

00:28:14.250 --> 00:28:17.790
So you don't need to worry about
how to craft a defense for it.

00:28:17.790 --> 00:28:20.250
As you can see, it's a single
rule which, in this case,

00:28:20.250 --> 00:28:21.744
as far as SQL injection.

00:28:21.744 --> 00:28:23.910
And there's a similar rule
for cross-site scripting.

00:28:23.910 --> 00:28:26.370
And then we'll continue
to add to this rule set

00:28:26.370 --> 00:28:28.080
so you don't have to
worry about crafting

00:28:28.080 --> 00:28:31.440
these rules on your end.

00:28:31.440 --> 00:28:33.870
The fourth one is what
you saw before when we

00:28:33.870 --> 00:28:37.140
did the nice Black Friday demo.

00:28:37.140 --> 00:28:39.120
In this case, what
you're essentially doing

00:28:39.120 --> 00:28:41.700
is taking a set of
parameters, and you are

00:28:41.700 --> 00:28:43.680
crafting your own custom rules.

00:28:43.680 --> 00:28:45.320
These could be for
day-zero attacks.

00:28:45.320 --> 00:28:48.480
These could be for customized
access for your services.

00:28:48.480 --> 00:28:50.910
And we have several
partners who are actually

00:28:50.910 --> 00:28:53.680
using this to build out
their own products, as well.

00:28:53.680 --> 00:28:56.580
So there are several uses
cases for this language.

00:28:56.580 --> 00:28:58.860
And in this case, it's
looking for traffic

00:28:58.860 --> 00:29:02.230
that's originating from US
where the user agent contains

00:29:02.230 --> 00:29:05.610
bad port and also the
cookie is a specific value.

00:29:05.610 --> 00:29:07.845
And it's likely going
to drop that traffic.

00:29:07.845 --> 00:29:08.720
NICK ORLOVE: Awesome.

00:29:08.720 --> 00:29:10.800
Yeah that's always
on everyone's mind

00:29:10.800 --> 00:29:12.638
is how do we prevent
zero-day attacks?

00:29:12.638 --> 00:29:13.513
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: Yeah.

00:29:13.513 --> 00:29:16.013
NICK ORLOVE: That is something
very difficult to accomplish.

00:29:16.013 --> 00:29:17.722
So I'm glad that
Google's gotten into it.

00:29:17.722 --> 00:29:19.304
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: Yeah,
this is actually

00:29:19.304 --> 00:29:20.460
building out the framework.

00:29:20.460 --> 00:29:22.140
The whole thing is,
anytime you want

00:29:22.140 --> 00:29:23.970
to defend against
an attack, you need

00:29:23.970 --> 00:29:27.245
to be able to craft these
really custom signatures.

00:29:27.245 --> 00:29:28.620
And to do that,
first of all, you

00:29:28.620 --> 00:29:31.590
need a very flexible language
where you can express what

00:29:31.590 --> 00:29:32.940
it is that you want to block.

00:29:32.940 --> 00:29:36.106
So this actually goes a
long way in doing that.

00:29:36.106 --> 00:29:37.425
NICK ORLOVE: Awesome.

00:29:37.425 --> 00:29:38.800
PRAJAKTA JOSHI:
One of the things

00:29:38.800 --> 00:29:41.350
is when you actually
do all of this,

00:29:41.350 --> 00:29:43.540
you do want to make
sure that you're

00:29:43.540 --> 00:29:46.120
able to see what is
blocked or what is allowed.

00:29:46.120 --> 00:29:49.070
And we show these
VR Stackdriver logs.

00:29:49.070 --> 00:29:51.610
So if you zoomed in on
this one, what it's doing

00:29:51.610 --> 00:29:54.640
is this was essentially
an SQL injection

00:29:54.640 --> 00:29:56.150
attack that was blocked.

00:29:56.150 --> 00:29:59.410
And it is telling you that
the action taken was denied.

00:29:59.410 --> 00:30:01.570
It tells you what
policy was matched.

00:30:01.570 --> 00:30:04.420
It also tells you the
priority, which basically maps

00:30:04.420 --> 00:30:06.040
to the rule that blocked it.

00:30:06.040 --> 00:30:07.960
And then it also
gives a string, which

00:30:07.960 --> 00:30:10.120
is denied by security
policy, which

00:30:10.120 --> 00:30:13.030
gives you some more additional
details about the block itself.

00:30:13.030 --> 00:30:15.910
So this is very critical
because a lot of customers

00:30:15.910 --> 00:30:18.160
would want to see what is
blocked and what is allowed,

00:30:18.160 --> 00:30:19.930
and then also to see
that the policies are

00:30:19.930 --> 00:30:20.890
working as intended.

00:30:23.570 --> 00:30:25.850
The last one is actually a
very interesting use case.

00:30:25.850 --> 00:30:30.110
And we have another product
called identity of their proxy

00:30:30.110 --> 00:30:32.820
and that will essentially
work with user identities

00:30:32.820 --> 00:30:34.980
and will tell you if the
traffic is authenticated.

00:30:34.980 --> 00:30:37.370
So can we access
a service or not?

00:30:37.370 --> 00:30:40.100
So it's basically
identity-based access control.

00:30:40.100 --> 00:30:43.910
The nice thing is that it
works with the HTTP/HTTPS load

00:30:43.910 --> 00:30:44.870
balancer.

00:30:44.870 --> 00:30:47.870
And so you can use it in
conjunction with Cloud Armor.

00:30:47.870 --> 00:30:50.900
So imagine you wanted to say
something to the effect of,

00:30:50.900 --> 00:30:53.840
only let the traffic
in if, let's say,

00:30:53.840 --> 00:30:58.850
the user is coming from US
and the user is authenticated

00:30:58.850 --> 00:31:01.970
or has been-- it's allowed
to access the service

00:31:01.970 --> 00:31:03.530
based on identity.

00:31:03.530 --> 00:31:05.870
And that is when you would
turn on both Cloud Armor

00:31:05.870 --> 00:31:07.580
and identity of error proxy.

00:31:07.580 --> 00:31:09.680
And it gives you a really
good set of, again,

00:31:09.680 --> 00:31:13.550
rules that you can deploy to
see what kind of access control

00:31:13.550 --> 00:31:14.460
you want to do.

00:31:14.460 --> 00:31:15.580
So you can layer on--

00:31:15.580 --> 00:31:17.120
like, think of it as the stack.

00:31:17.120 --> 00:31:19.550
You've got layer three,
layer four, layer seven.

00:31:19.550 --> 00:31:21.890
And with identity of error
proxy working in conjunction

00:31:21.890 --> 00:31:24.590
with Cloud Armor,
you have a layer x

00:31:24.590 --> 00:31:26.760
on top, which is
essentially identity.

00:31:26.760 --> 00:31:29.600
So it's a very powerful stack
that you have at your disposal.

00:31:29.600 --> 00:31:29.840
NICK ORLOVE: Yeah.

00:31:29.840 --> 00:31:31.881
I can definitely see how
this would be incredibly

00:31:31.881 --> 00:31:34.370
applicable for companies
that might have employees who

00:31:34.370 --> 00:31:37.599
are going on the road or getting
into airplanes, traveling,

00:31:37.599 --> 00:31:39.890
et cetera, because it would
be really difficult to say,

00:31:39.890 --> 00:31:42.830
all right, this employee,
only accept their IP address

00:31:42.830 --> 00:31:44.590
when they're in California.

00:31:44.590 --> 00:31:46.340
But when they go
on a sales trip,

00:31:46.340 --> 00:31:48.830
it would be difficult to
change the rules automatically.

00:31:48.830 --> 00:31:51.615
So the combination of
Cloud Armor plus identity

00:31:51.615 --> 00:31:54.474
of error proxy just makes
it incredibly powerful.

00:31:54.474 --> 00:31:55.640
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: That's true.

00:31:55.640 --> 00:31:56.184
That's true.

00:31:56.184 --> 00:31:58.100
NICK ORLOVE: So I hear
you have a demo for us.

00:31:58.100 --> 00:31:59.141
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: So, yeah.

00:31:59.141 --> 00:32:01.850
Because I knew you would ask me
a question, so I have it ready.

00:32:01.850 --> 00:32:05.240
So what I wanted to
do is we showed you

00:32:05.240 --> 00:32:07.370
what it looks like from
a customer's perspective

00:32:07.370 --> 00:32:09.230
using the Black Friday example.

00:32:09.230 --> 00:32:11.390
We showed you some of
the constructs that

00:32:11.390 --> 00:32:13.370
actually make up Cloud Armor.

00:32:13.370 --> 00:32:15.640
What I wanted to show you
is a demo and then also

00:32:15.640 --> 00:32:17.390
show you a little bit
of the configuration

00:32:17.390 --> 00:32:18.840
or under the hood.

00:32:18.840 --> 00:32:21.520
So I thought we should
pick-- let's pick the three

00:32:21.520 --> 00:32:23.900
typical attacks
that you can expect

00:32:23.900 --> 00:32:25.830
customers to be worried about.

00:32:25.830 --> 00:32:30.640
And so let's go back in here.

00:32:30.640 --> 00:32:31.450
OK.

00:32:31.450 --> 00:32:34.510
So the first one essentially
is the DDoS attack.

00:32:34.510 --> 00:32:37.420
Now, in this case, the
way we have set it up

00:32:37.420 --> 00:32:39.050
is we have two projects.

00:32:39.050 --> 00:32:40.720
One is the attacker project.

00:32:40.720 --> 00:32:42.910
One is the defender project.

00:32:42.910 --> 00:32:44.560
In the attacker project--

00:32:44.560 --> 00:32:46.660
and we have set up this
tool called Metasploit

00:32:46.660 --> 00:32:50.260
and some of our own home grown
systems to go launch attacks.

00:32:50.260 --> 00:32:52.322
The other side in
the defender project

00:32:52.322 --> 00:32:54.280
is where we'll go and
configure a set of things

00:32:54.280 --> 00:32:56.020
to defend against the attacks.

00:32:56.020 --> 00:32:58.730
So the first one is an
infrastructure DDoS attack.

00:32:58.730 --> 00:33:00.880
Now, let's go to
the defense side.

00:33:00.880 --> 00:33:03.340
If you notice the red
thing, that is basically

00:33:03.340 --> 00:33:04.540
your attack traffic.

00:33:04.540 --> 00:33:06.160
And you can see that things--

00:33:06.160 --> 00:33:07.772
like, you can see
that line go up.

00:33:07.772 --> 00:33:10.230
NICK ORLOVE: And these are real
data points, not anything--

00:33:10.230 --> 00:33:10.840
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: Yes.

00:33:10.840 --> 00:33:12.800
And I'll show you the
stuff behind the good.

00:33:12.800 --> 00:33:13.390
NICK ORLOVE: That's cool.

00:33:13.390 --> 00:33:15.520
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: Now, if
you look at this one,

00:33:15.520 --> 00:33:17.440
if you look in here,
all we need to do

00:33:17.440 --> 00:33:20.270
for this one is turn on
the global HTTP/HTTPS load

00:33:20.270 --> 00:33:21.040
balancer.

00:33:21.040 --> 00:33:22.600
That's all this command does.

00:33:22.600 --> 00:33:26.020
So when I click it, you can
notice that the load balancer

00:33:26.020 --> 00:33:27.037
got deployed.

00:33:27.037 --> 00:33:28.870
And you can see that
the line is essentially

00:33:28.870 --> 00:33:31.840
going down, which means our
attack traffic is going down.

00:33:31.840 --> 00:33:33.670
It's now almost mitigated.

00:33:33.670 --> 00:33:35.830
The second attack, if you
remember we spoke about,

00:33:35.830 --> 00:33:37.340
was SQL injection.

00:33:37.340 --> 00:33:39.160
Now again, this
is using the tool.

00:33:39.160 --> 00:33:42.580
This is not as much about
volume but essentially just

00:33:42.580 --> 00:33:45.680
exploiting the
SQL set of traffic

00:33:45.680 --> 00:33:47.800
and then injecting a
malicious stuff in there.

00:33:47.800 --> 00:33:50.810
So again, Metasploit generates
an SQL injection attack.

00:33:50.810 --> 00:33:54.110
And on the defense side, this
is where Cloud Armor kicks in.

00:33:54.110 --> 00:33:57.460
So when we click that button,
what it's going to deploy

00:33:57.460 --> 00:34:01.480
is a security policy, which is
turning on essentially the SQL

00:34:01.480 --> 00:34:03.010
injection defense.

00:34:03.010 --> 00:34:05.210
So let's turn that on.

00:34:05.210 --> 00:34:10.340
And you can notice that the
attack traffic has gone down.

00:34:10.340 --> 00:34:13.264
The last one is the one that
we spoke about earlier, which

00:34:13.264 --> 00:34:14.389
is the multi-vector attack.

00:34:14.389 --> 00:34:17.090
So you could have either
infrastructure in this

00:34:17.090 --> 00:34:19.370
or you could have
different types of attacks

00:34:19.370 --> 00:34:22.639
within a single attack
or from a single entity.

00:34:22.639 --> 00:34:26.080
And let's launch
this attack in here.

00:34:26.080 --> 00:34:28.820
Let's assume that this attack,
the signature of the attack

00:34:28.820 --> 00:34:32.389
is traffic coming in from
US with user-agent bad bot

00:34:32.389 --> 00:34:34.800
and then a specific
type of cookie.

00:34:34.800 --> 00:34:37.129
So we would put in a
rule that actually does

00:34:37.129 --> 00:34:39.170
these three things, looks
for these three things,

00:34:39.170 --> 00:34:40.949
and it denies the traffic.

00:34:40.949 --> 00:34:43.710
So again, the red is
the attacker traffic.

00:34:43.710 --> 00:34:46.820
And when I deny, this you can
see that the attack traffic

00:34:46.820 --> 00:34:47.480
is going down.

00:34:47.480 --> 00:34:49.520
So the attack is mitigated.

00:34:49.520 --> 00:34:52.250
And just to show that
this is all real,

00:34:52.250 --> 00:34:55.560
let me show you the project
that sits behind this.

00:34:55.560 --> 00:34:58.340
So this is the defender
side of things.

00:34:58.340 --> 00:34:59.780
These are our Stackdriver logs.

00:34:59.780 --> 00:35:02.480
So when you look in
here, you can see

00:35:02.480 --> 00:35:04.920
what is getting logged in here.

00:35:04.920 --> 00:35:07.480
And then you know basically
what happened, et cetera.

00:35:07.480 --> 00:35:10.080
So this is where you look at
those logs that we spoke about.

00:35:10.080 --> 00:35:11.510
And then the actual
configuration

00:35:11.510 --> 00:35:15.320
would be somewhere in here where
if you go to the networking

00:35:15.320 --> 00:35:18.230
side of things, this
is security policies

00:35:18.230 --> 00:35:20.767
is where all of these policies
are actually configured.

00:35:20.767 --> 00:35:22.850
And you know, there's a
bunch of interesting stuff

00:35:22.850 --> 00:35:27.530
to do in there, especially if
you start configuring rules.

00:35:27.530 --> 00:35:29.600
When we spoke about
the rules language,

00:35:29.600 --> 00:35:31.015
this is the advanced mode.

00:35:31.015 --> 00:35:33.140
And this is where you would
go and use the language

00:35:33.140 --> 00:35:34.327
to create your rules.

00:35:34.327 --> 00:35:36.410
NICK ORLOVE: Man, talk
about real time protection.

00:35:36.410 --> 00:35:38.110
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: Yeah.

00:35:38.110 --> 00:35:43.100
So this is actually where--

00:35:43.100 --> 00:35:45.090
you know, it
basically is the time

00:35:45.090 --> 00:35:47.000
where I ask you the question.

00:35:47.000 --> 00:35:49.640
And I say, Nick, so what do
you think of Cloud Armor?

00:35:49.640 --> 00:35:52.630
NICK ORLOVE: I think it's an
incredibly powerful product.

00:35:52.630 --> 00:35:55.160
It is definitely one
of the amazing tools

00:35:55.160 --> 00:36:01.160
that we can put in our customers
toolbelt, security protection

00:36:01.160 --> 00:36:02.420
tools.

00:36:02.420 --> 00:36:05.900
And so what I really took away
was four key things, first

00:36:05.900 --> 00:36:09.170
being Cloud Armor does a
phenomenal job of mitigating

00:36:09.170 --> 00:36:11.785
DDoS attacks, whether
that's just a TCP SYN

00:36:11.785 --> 00:36:16.460
flood, amplification attacks,
or fragmentation attacks,

00:36:16.460 --> 00:36:18.290
we really have our
customers' back

00:36:18.290 --> 00:36:20.670
and make sure that
they're protected.

00:36:20.670 --> 00:36:25.850
Also, I love that the complex
or the comprehensive rules

00:36:25.850 --> 00:36:28.340
allow you to protect on all
sorts of different areas,

00:36:28.340 --> 00:36:31.610
whether it's a specific to
IP address, geolocation,

00:36:31.610 --> 00:36:35.000
custom matches, L3
through L7, it's

00:36:35.000 --> 00:36:38.360
very enabling to give
this to our customers

00:36:38.360 --> 00:36:40.970
and let them create and
set their own rules.

00:36:40.970 --> 00:36:43.820
Also, I really like that
this not only depends

00:36:43.820 --> 00:36:47.835
on the infrastructure attacks,
but also application of attacks

00:36:47.835 --> 00:36:50.600
so that Google supports our
customers in their applications

00:36:50.600 --> 00:36:52.130
that they're trying to run.

00:36:52.130 --> 00:36:55.061
And then lastly,
what solution would

00:36:55.061 --> 00:36:57.560
exist without some kind of way
of monitoring and making sure

00:36:57.560 --> 00:36:59.790
that what you've deployed
is actually working?

00:36:59.790 --> 00:37:02.480
So I love that this is real time
and we can enable our customers

00:37:02.480 --> 00:37:03.320
to do that.

00:37:03.320 --> 00:37:04.760
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: Perfect.

00:37:04.760 --> 00:37:07.520
That's good.

00:37:07.520 --> 00:37:10.820
