NICK ORLOVE: OK.

00:42:46.650 --> 00:42:48.985
And can you share any
details around performance?

00:42:48.985 --> 00:42:49.860
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: Sure.

00:42:49.860 --> 00:42:52.260
So one of the things
where we always

00:42:52.260 --> 00:42:55.500
do well on all of Google
Cloud Services is performance.

00:42:55.500 --> 00:42:57.660
And the reason for that
is these are architected

00:42:57.660 --> 00:42:59.470
to be distributed systems.

00:42:59.470 --> 00:43:02.280
And so if you think of
the global HTTP/HTTPS load

00:43:02.280 --> 00:43:06.240
balancer, we published a
blog post two years back

00:43:06.240 --> 00:43:09.610
where we tested it with about
a million queries per second,

00:43:09.610 --> 00:43:10.710
which is huge.

00:43:10.710 --> 00:43:14.050
And beyond that, we
stopped, because that's

00:43:14.050 --> 00:43:17.490
way more than what a typical
customer can expect to see.

00:43:17.490 --> 00:43:18.990
The same applies
for Cloud Armor,

00:43:18.990 --> 00:43:21.770
as well, because it works with
the global HTTP/HTTPS load

00:43:21.770 --> 00:43:22.810
balancer.

00:43:22.810 --> 00:43:25.080
And so the global
HTTP/HTTPS performance,

00:43:25.080 --> 00:43:27.480
again, is applicable to
Cloud Armor, as well.

